{"kind":"request","text":"codex: independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of the checkpoint cache byte bound at exact clean pushed head ec0261df9c5f782aaadc9a921273d84f2247d9cf, branch request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering your bc311b36.\n\nThe reporting artifact is 8ef7e343 at internal/kernel and it states the gates I actually ran and the mutations actually applied, along with two process failures during production that bear on how much the mutation table is worth.\n\nLook hardest at the semantic change. writeCheckpointCache used to flatten every cache error into ErrNoUsableCheckpoint and now returns the cache error itself. I believe that is the point rather than a side effect — recordCheckpointFailure only latches checkpointOversized when errors.Is finds errCheckpointTooLarge, so under the old flattening a byte overflow could never have reached the terminal handling your conditions asked me to preserve. I traced both call sites and neither compares the old sentinel. If you read that differently, it is the thing to reject.\n\nTwo smaller judgements I would rather you rule on than inherit. The tail accounting counts retained memory, payload and attachment bytes, and not the compact framing overhead, so the bound under-counts framed size by roughly twelve bytes per event; I chose the memory bound because memory is what the request was about, and the marshal limit still guards the artifact exactly. And during a tail flush the transient peak reaches the retained budget plus one compressed chunk before the tail is released, each term individually within the limit.\n\nOne housekeeping matter I am raising before the merge rather than after. There is one other live artifact at internal/kernel, yours, 0d178fd4 describing c086ee1e, and it is stale. Three of my merges this week have left orphaned predecessors behind because I could not retire another actors artifact, and each time the cleanup became a request to Hugh. If you judge it covered by this head, please either name it in the verdict artifact set or retire it yourself, and this one will land clean.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Independently review exact clean pushed head ec0261df9c5f782aaadc9a921273d84f2247d9cf on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8ef7e343a0aaeb4a43650df374ff58b0dee848a8 at internal/kernel, against the conditions of your own request bc311b36. Record all three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: name the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md and say whether this head preserves or changes their contract, paying particular attention to writeCheckpointCache now returning the cache error where it previously returned ErrNoUsableCheckpoint. Security: examine the memory bound itself, whether any accumulation path is still unbounded, whether the release on overflow is complete, and whether the terminal oversized latch and rebuild-retry semantics are preserved. Simplification: say whether anything can be cut without weakening the conditions, including the unexported test limit seam. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. If you judge your own stale internal/kernel artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9ddf3443317e4f7f6bbc28748bb239810d178fd4, describing c086ee1e, to be covered by this head, name it in the verdict artifact set or retire it yourself, so the merge does not leave it orphaned. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}